<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      Without this, Shiver cannot see that a distributor is installed at all.

      Android 11 stopped letting an app enumerate the others on the phone: `queryBroadcastReceivers`
      returns only packages already visible to the caller, so Shiver's search for anything answering
      the UnifiedPush register broadcast came back empty with ntfy sitting right there, and the
      settings screen said "install a distributor" to someone who had one.

      A `<queries>` element declaring the exact intent is the sanctioned way back in, and it is
      narrow on purpose: it makes visible the apps that answer *this* action, and nothing else on
      the device. `QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES` would do it too and is both a permission Shiver has no business
      holding and grounds for removal from the Play Store.
    -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="org.unifiedpush.android.distributor.REGISTER" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

    <application>
        <!--
          The cold-start receiver. Declared here rather than registered in code because that is the
          whole point: Android must be able to deliver a push to Shiver when Shiver is not running, and
          only a manifest receiver can be started into a dead process.

          **Exported, and it has to be.** It was not, on the reasoning that the distributor targets
          Shiver's package directly so nothing else needs to reach it. That is not how Android works:
          `exported="false"` means *no other app* may deliver here, package-targeted or not, and the
          distributor is another app. The result was a registration that looked fine from ntfy's
          side — the topic was created — while Shiver never received the endpoint it was sent, so the
          settings screen sat on "0 of 2 servers registered" forever and no push ever arrived.

          What exporting costs is that any app on the phone can broadcast at it. What stops that
          mattering is the token: Shiver only acts on tokens it issued, and they are the rail entries'
          own v4 uuids. A broadcast carrying anything else is dropped without a notification, which
          is the check `onReceive` starts with.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name="com.shiver.push.PushReceiver"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="org.unifiedpush.android.connector.MESSAGE" />
                <action android:name="org.unifiedpush.android.connector.NEW_ENDPOINT" />
                <action android:name="org.unifiedpush.android.connector.REGISTRATION_FAILED" />
                <action android:name="org.unifiedpush.android.connector.UNREGISTERED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>
    </application>
</manifest>
